.TH DRAW3DBENCH 1
.SH NAME
draw3dbench \- compare the software and protocol draw3d providers
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B draw3dbench
[
.B -p
] [
.B -n
.I frames
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Draw3dbench
draws the same scene through both providers of the
.IR draw3d (2)
interface \-
.B /dis/math/draw3d.dis
(software: projection and rasterization in Limbo) and
.B /dis/math/draw3ddev.dis
(the
.IR draw (3)
protocol, which on a Metal host does the transform and the fill on the
GPU) \- and reports milliseconds per frame and the number of lit pixels.
.PP
It varies two things that are not obvious from reading either provider.
.PP
The first is the destination.
The Metal geometry path refuses any destination that is not the screen
image or a fully clear layer of it, so a program that composes its frame
into an offscreen buffer and blits gets no GPU at all, and through the
protocol provider pays the wire cost on top of the software one.
Both destinations are timed.
.PP
The second is triangle size.
Per-call overhead and per-pixel cost pull in opposite directions, so a
scene of 512 small triangles and one of 64 wall-sized triangles are
separate measurements.
.PP
.PP
.I Sprite2d
is timed separately, into both destinations, because it is what the
appl/temple ports lean on and it is the other half of what
.IR draw3d (2)
is asked for.
Queueing a GPU sprite is nearly free and the drawing happens at
presentation, so timing the calls alone would report zero and mean
nothing; every batch therefore ends with a read of one row of the
display's screen image, which runs the readback hook and waits for the
GPU.
That is once per batch because it is once per frame in a real port, and
the software rows pay the same read.
Into the window, at 640\(mu480:
.PP
.EX
                          software   protocol
16\(mu16 upright                 0 us        0 us
64\(mu64 upright                 5 us        5 us
220\(mu220 upright              45 us       45 us
220\(mu220 at 30 degrees      2725 us       65 us
64\(mu64 scaled to 220        6690 us       50 us
.EE
.PP
The upright rows are equal on purpose:
.I sprite2d
hands the device only the calls that need resampling, since a sprite
drawn upright at its own size is a plain masked
.I draw
and nothing beats that.
Handing those to the GPU as well measured 60 us against 5 us for a
64\(mu64, because the sprite queue is drained by presentation and a
tight loop of cheap sprites throttles to the present rate.
.PP
The last two sections are correctness rather than speed.
One reports where the pixels a GPU pass produced can be read back from.
The other draws a red, a green and a blue triangle at known world
positions through both providers and checks that they land in the same
place, cover the same number of pixels, and are dominated by the same
colour component \- the two bugs that kept the Metal geometry path from
ever drawing were one of each, a clip that threw the whole scene away
and a channel order that turned red into blue, and neither would have
shown up in a timing.
It reads the display's own screen image with
.IR readpixels ,
which is the only way to see GPU output from inside
.IR emu ,
and it does not flush first: a present would drain the GPU's queue and
leave the readback nothing to replay.
.PP
What it checks is
.I geometry ,
not colour: the Metal fragment shader shades per pixel and the software
rasteriser gives a face one flat colour, so the two are meant to differ
in shade.
The mean colour of each triangle is printed for both, so the difference
is visible \- a face-on red triangle reads 255,0,0 through the software
provider and about 253,17,17 through the GPU, the extra being the
specular lobe.
Setting
.B INFERNO_METAL_FLAT
turns the shading off, and then the two agree exactly; that is the
control to run if a difference here is ever suspected of being more
than shading.
.PP
Between them is a one-number summary of what the fragment shader is
worth: how many distinct colours a
.I single
face comes out in.
.I Fillpoly3
hands a face one colour, so the software rasteriser fills it with that
colour and no other \- one \- while the GPU lights it per pixel and the
view vector varies across the face.
The triangle is steep, running from near the eye to far from it, because
a face-on one has almost no variation in V and would under-report.
Counting distinct colours rather than measuring a range keeps the answer
independent of which lighting model is in the shader: software 1, GPU
16 at the 18-bit quantisation the test uses and 63 at full precision.
With
.B INFERNO_METAL_FLAT
set the GPU reads 1 as well, which is the control.
.PP
The last section checks how a GPU sprite composites at the edge of its
mask, through
.IR sprite3zb ,
which
.B /appl/wm/draw3ddemo.b
calls and no
.B appl/temple
port does.
Both cases magnify a small sprite four times, so the sampler is
certainly filtering, and both use a metric that a resampler cannot fake
because it is one-directional.
.TF "bleed"
.TP
.B halo
A white sprite, black outside its mask, on a green ground.
Blending white over green cannot lower the green channel below 255
whatever weight the filter picks, so the smallest green byte in the
frame is 255 when the compositing is right and less when it is not.
.TP
.B bleed
A white sprite, red outside its mask, on a black ground.
The invisible red must stay invisible, so the largest amount by which
red exceeds green is 0 when the hidden colour is not being mixed in.
.PD
.PP
The software provider is the control for both: it blits through a binary
mask with no filter, and reads 255 and 0.
.PP
The
.B Sprite3
family \-
.IR sprite3 ,
.I sprite3zb
and
.I sprite3yb
\- is checked rather than timed.
Its only caller is
.IR draw3ddemo (1),
which draws two sprites beside a cube and looks plausible doing it, so
until this check existed nothing had established that the family was
right \- which is not the same as nothing calling it.
Six properties are reported per provider: that a sprite lands where
.I project
says its world point does; that its on-screen width is 16, 32 and 8
pixels at z of \-4, \-2 and \-8; that
.I sprite3zb
at 90 degrees turns a 48\(mu12 sprite into a 12\(mu48 one; that
.I sprite3yb
comes out 0.85 of the height
.I sprite3
gives the same sprite; that with the z-buffer on, a sprite behind a
nearer face contributes no pixels while the same sprite in front of it
does; and that of two sprites drawn at the same depth in the same place,
all of the second survives.
.PP
Two of those are shaped the way they are because the obvious shape passes
for the wrong reason.
Shrinking a sprite cannot test a resample, since
.I draw
clips rather than resamples and a 16-wide sprite cropped to 8 is 8 wide
exactly as a resampled one is; only enlarging discriminates.
And zero occluded pixels is also what a provider that drew no sprite at
all would report, so the sprite in front is not decoration, it is what
makes the zero mean occlusion.
.PP
The same-depth case is there because every sprite has one depth apiece
and
.BR GR_Z_ALL ,
in the original, puts whole groups of them at the same one.
A strict depth test therefore makes the second sprite of an overlapping
pair invisible, which is the ordinary case in a scene and not a corner of
it; equal-wins resolves them in draw order.
Made strict again, the check reports 0 of 256 pixels surviving.
.PP
The options are:
.TF "-n frames"
.TP
.B -p
Sleep 16ms per frame, so that presentation is not the limit and
.B INFERNO_METAL_STATS
prints its per-30-frame counters during the run
.TP
.BI -n " frames"
Frames per case (default 120)
.PD
.PP
Two environment variables matter to what it measures:
.TF INFERNO_METAL_STATS
.TP
.B INFERNO_METAL_STATS
Print the Metal counters every 30 presents
.TP
.B INFERNO_METAL_FLAT
Turn off the per-pixel shading in the Metal geometry shader, so the GPU
reproduces the software rasteriser's flat per-face colour exactly
.PD
.SH EXAMPLE
Confirm that geometry really reaches the GPU rather than falling back:
.IP
.EX
INFERNO_METAL_STATS=1 emu-cocoa -r. /dis/draw3dbench.dis -p
.EE
.PP
.B g3_calls
is nonzero only while the protocol cases run, and
.B soft_fallbacks
stays at zero.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/draw3dbench.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR draw3d (2),
.IR polyfill (2),
.IR draw (3)
.SH BUGS
A Metal geometry pass can be read back, but only through a
.IR readpixels ,
never through a
.IR draw .
The 64-triangle scene is looked at twice for that reason: a
.I draw
into a scratch image copies bytes and never gives the device a chance to
bring GPU output back, while a
.I readpixels
straight off the target goes through the draw device's read message,
which calls the readback hook first.
The software provider agrees between the two \- the window layer reads
331763 lit pixels whichever way it is asked \- but the protocol
provider reads 0 through the
.I draw
and 1280370 through the
.IR readpixels .
So anything that copies a layer's bytes rather than reading its pixels
will see a GPU-drawn frame as empty.
.PP
The two mask-edge cases use different sprite sizes on purpose.
.IR emu 's
sprite-texture cache is keyed on the image pointer, its data pointer and
its size, and on nothing about its contents, so a sprite freed and
another allocated straight afterwards can land on the same addresses and
the second draw silently uses the first's texture.
That is fixed \- every image free now drops the cache entries naming it
\- but the key is still content-blind, and two same-sized sprites alive
at once and swapped in place would still collide.
.PP
.I Sprite3mat
is not checked.
Neither provider applies the matrix it is handed: both take its
translation by transforming the origin through it and discard the
rotation and scale entirely, so there is nothing yet for a check to
distinguish.
.PP
At exactly
.B -n 60
the 512-triangle protocol case reports 8.4ms a frame instead of 1.5,
because of a single 414ms stall in one frame.
It is absent at 10, 20, 40, 50, 55 and 120 frames, reproducible at 60,
and decays through 65, 80 and 100.
The cause is not known.
The obvious suspect is the fixed 49152-vertex ring that holds geometry
the presenter has not yet drawn \-
.I Flushnow
does not drain it, and the submission call spins until the main thread
catches up \- but saturation would get worse with more frames, not
better, so that is not what this is.
Sweep
.B -n
before quoting any per-frame figure.
.PP
Only
.I fillpoly3
is measured for speed, and only it is checked for agreement between the
providers.
.IR line3 ,
.I plot3
and
.I fillpoly3g
have their own paths in both providers and are not covered at all.
The
.I sprite3
family is checked but not timed, and its checks are per provider: they
say each provider is self-consistent, not that the two draw the same
pixels.
.PP
The agreement check is occasionally flaky in the other direction: a
present that lands between the drawing and the read drains the GPU's
geometry queue, the readback then has nothing to replay, and the frame
reports
.B "nothing drawn"
when it drew perfectly well.
Re-run it; two clean runs out of three is the pattern, and a real
failure repeats.
